        }
      },
      "snippet": {
        "text": "The discussion surrounding generative AI as a powerful tool for learning and teaching often draws attention to teacher agency. Some researchers argue that generative AI has the potential to enhance teacher agency by providing tools for more personalised teaching (Jeon and Lee 2023;Koraishi 2023). For example, Jeon and Lee (2023) state that 'with ChatGPT, teachers would now have significantly more control over creating and revising lesson materials than in the past, when they had access to fewer teaching resources and had to manually develop and revise materials (p. 15885). Similarly, Koraishi (2023) argues that integration of AI technologies can 'empower EFL teachers to optimize their teaching effectiveness and better address the multifaceted needs of their learners ' (p. 56). \n\nAt the same time, generative AI is often seen as challenging teachers' agency. Research has found that many teachers' lack of familiarity with the educational affordances of these tools, feel uncertain about how to use AI effectively and have difficulties in differentiating between student work and AI output (Jeon and Lee 2023;Kohnke et al. 2023a;Lan & Chen, 2024). Furthermore, as AI helps to automate some aspects of teaching and learning, it brings into focus the evolving role of teachers, challenging traditional notions of authority and expertise. However, the evolving nature of teacher roles, in light of emerging technologies, is not new (Ally 2019) but reflects the complex relationship between new technologies and the nature of teachers' roles. Therefore, the challenges associated with generative AI, as one of the newest developments, alongside dominant discourses about the future of AI in education (e.g. AI replacing teachers), has the potential to significantly affect teachers' agency by undermining their confidence, reducing autonomy to make decisions and limiting their capacity to exercise professional judgement (Aleman et al., 2024). \n\nHowever, researchers note that the availability of helpful resources generated by AI does not necessarily improve the quality of instruction, emphasising the importance of teachers' nuanced understanding of their students and contexts, professional knowledge and judgement when creating with and using AI within their instructional contexts (Jeon and Lee 2023;Koraishi 2023).",
