        }
      },
      "snippet": {
        "text": "In terms of lesson planning, experienced teachers pay attention to an optimal fit between the learning level of the pupils and the learning content (König & Rothland, 2022). However, student teachers, pre-service teachers, and teachers at early career stages (e.g., also lateral entrants) often still lack the experience and routine to plan lessons adaptively and structure learning units over a longer period of time. In addition, the selection of suitable learning materials can also pose a challenge when preparing lessons. ChatGPT can be used here as a digital assistance tool to support prospective teachers in organizational terms, as initial studies from the USA, the UK, Turkey, and Australia describe (Gurl et al. this issue;Baytak, 2024;Karaman & Göksu, 2024;Cameron & Mesiti, 2024), even if the quality of the created lesson plans still varied depending on the model. \n\nIn his study, Baytak (2024) conducted a content analysis of lesson plans generated by ChatGPT and Google Gemini. The study found that the lesson plans created by ChatGPT and Gemini were similar in structure to human-written plans, including clear learning objectives, detailed activities, and assessments. ChatGPT, in particular, demonstrated the ability to assist in creating comprehensive lesson plans that incorporated a variety of teaching strategies and resources. It could suggest activities, exercises, and examples to illustrate mathematical concepts, in addition to structuring lessons, incorporating interactive activities, and aligning with curriculum standards. Moreover, ChatGPT offered teachers the flexibility to customize lesson plans to suit the varying learning paces and styles of their students, enhancing the accessibility of mathematical concepts for all learners. \n\nThis adaptability underscores ChatGPT's potential as a valuable tool for supporting teachers in planning and delivering effective, student-centered lessons (Wardat et al., 2023). Also, in other studies, ChatGPT was found to support differentiated instruction by suggesting modifications and accommodations within lesson plans to cater to students with varying abilities and learning styles. This ensured that all students were engaged and challenged at their own levels (Gurl et al. this issue).",
