      "snippet": {
        "text": "Generative AI technologies such as OpenAI's GPT-4 have rapidly entered the education landscape, raising questions about how they can support or augment human instructors. Early evidence in academic settings shows that AI tools like ChatGPT can reduce teachers' workload in tasks like lesson planning, allowing educators to generate quizzes, brainstorm activities, and customize materials more efficiently (Baxter,2024). In one trial, teachers who used ChatGPT cut their planning time by about 31% compared to peers who did not use AI. Such productivity gains illustrate what AI can do well -e.g. automate routine content creation -freeing human teachers to focus on higher-level pedagogical work (Kuka & Sabitzer, 2024). At the same time, there is caution that AI outputs must be vetted for quality and that the irreplaceable aspects of teaching (such as mentorship and ethical judgment) remain with humans (Pashaie, Mohammadi, & Golmohammadi, 2024). This balance is especially pertinent in fitness education, a domain that combines cognitive learning with physical, embodied practice. \n\nIn fields like sports coaching and physical training, AI is beginning to be used for data analysis and personalized training programs. For instance, AI systems can analyze athletes' performance metrics or even video of movements to provide feedback or suggest improvements. However, coaching research emphasizes that the \"human touch\"leadership, motivation, and individualized support -is critical for athlete development. AI might excel at crunching data or suggesting optimizations, but maintaining the human elements of empathy, creativity, and ethical oversight remains a \"critical challenge\" in integration. Pashaie et al. (2024) describe a \"Synergy Theory\" in sports coaching, wherein AI enhances training outcomes only when combined with human-centered practices, cautioning against over-reliance on AI that could undermine the coach-athlete relationship. This resonates with broader debates on whether AI should be viewed as just a tool or as a collaborative teammate in professional work. Some argue that even advanced AI should not be anthropomorphized as a teammate, as it lacks genuine understanding or agency.",
