      "snippet": {
        "text": "Generative Artificial Intelligence (GenAI) refers to a subset of AI technologies designed to create high-quality new content based on existing data. These systems can generate human-like text, create images, compose music, and develop code by analyzing large datasets (nikolopoulou 2024). GenAI functions by learning patterns, structures, and relationships within the data on which it is trained, enabling it to produce contextually relevant outputs that are often indistinguishable from those created by humans (Hennekeuser et al. 2024). Its ability to perform complex tasks at unprecedented speeds makes GenAI useful across various domains, including healthcare, finance, creative industries, and educational settings (nikolopoulou 2024). Among the most prominent applications of GenAI in education is the use of AI chatbots. These are advanced software applications designed to interact with users through natural language processing (nLP), simulating human-like conversation (Fryer, nakao, and Thompson 2019;Labadze, Grigolia, and Machaidze 2023). Technological advancements have made AI chatbots more convenient, natural, and user-friendly, thereby expanding their potential to support educational practices in higher education (Zawacki-Richter et al. 2019;Usher and Amzalag 2025). By analyzing user inputs and drawing on extensive datasets, AI chatbots can engage in meaningful, context-aware conversations, offering tailored responses that cater to the individual needs of students and educators (Fryer, nakao, and Thompson 2019;Okonkwo and Ade-Ibijola 2021). \n\nThe integration of GenAI, particularly through AI chatbots, holds substantial benefits for higher education. These tools are capable of tailoring content to individual learning styles and paces, automating routine tasks, and offering innovative methods for assessing student work (Okonkwo and Ade-Ibijola 2021;Labadze, Grigolia, and Machaidze 2023). They efficiently respond to student inquiries in real time, assisting with various academic activities such as clarifying coursework and recommending resources (Usher and Amzalag 2025).",
