      "snippet": {
        "text": "The use of AI as a teaching and learning technology has a long history in education, most notably through intelligent tutoring systems (ITS) designed to foster reasoning, practice, and reflection rather than simply deliver answers [8,10,23,29]. These systems emphasized scaffolding through guided practice and feedback, with frameworks such as cognitive apprenticeship [10] and ICAP [8] showing that active engagement and reflection are critical to durable learning. This Manuscript submitted to ACM work established AI not only as a source of information but also as a partner that shapes how learners reason and practice. \n\nThe LLM has more recently been studied as a teaching and learning technology with mixed results. EducationQ [39] evaluates instructional effectiveness across models, showing that teaching quality does not scale linearly with size; PACE [26] simulates learner profiles and adapts Socratic feedback to personalize mathematics tutoring; and Agent4Edu [17] uses profile and memory modules to simulate learner responses for large-scale evaluation. These studies highlight the potential of LLMs for interaction and personalization, yet most LLMs remain optimized to provide direct answers rather than to scaffold reasoning or reflection. Their actual effects on learning outcomes, and the design choices needed to make them pedagogically effective, remain open questions. \n\nAt the same time, generative AI applications such as ChatGPT are already being adopted as teaching and learning tools across contexts. Students use them for studying and homework, often as a supplement to traditional resources [40]. \n\nInstructors adopt them for lesson preparation and teaching support, though surveys report ambivalence and uneven patterns of uptake [5,6,31]. Beyond formal education, adults in the workforce are using generative AI for professional learning, such as developing new skills, practicing workplace communication, and pursuing upskilling and reskilling opportunities [35,36]. These trends show that generative AI is making learning accessible to a wide range of learners, while also raising new challenges and opportunities for the design of systems that act not only as problem solvers but as effective pedagogical partners. This motivates our work on TeaPT, which examines how conversational design choices can make LLMs more effective for learning.",
