          "status": null,
          "disclaimer": "Notice: This snippet is extracted from the open access paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06411, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."
        }
      },
      "snippet": {
        "text": "Recent advances in generative AI have opened new opportunities for supporting teachers in instructional planning, assessment design, and classroom adaptation (Kwid et al., 2024). High-capacity LLMs such as GPT-4 have been positioned as general-purpose assistants for generating lesson plans and formative assessments tailored to specific educational contexts (Cooper, 2023). However, teachers often struggle to prompt these models effectively, frequently relying on zero-shot interactions and investing significant time validating and revising outputs (Dilling and Herrmann, 2024; Kuusemets et al., 2024). \n\nTo address these challenges, educational AI platforms have begun offering more structured support (Niu et al., 2022;Eden et al., 2024). For example, Khanmigo provides lesson-planning and contentgeneration tools (Khan Academy, n.d.), though these often follow rigid templates (Sawyer and Aga, 2024;Walkington, 2025). MagicSchool offers broader customization, such as generating questions from YouTube videos (Foster et al., 2024), yet few systems align with specific instructional goals or student experiences in third-party simulations (Dai and Ke, 2022). Despite these structured tools, most platforms remain limited in generating questions that are both pedagogically aligned and responsive to the dynamic content of virtual interactive simulations, highlighting the need for more targeted, flexible approaches. \n\nOne promising direction that addresses this gap is automated question generation. Prior work explores LLM-assisted generation of learner-adaptive, context-aware questions (Hsiao and Chung, 2022;Guo et al., 2022), including personalized feedback (Das et al., 2021), curriculum-aligned assessment (Zhang et al., 2022;Song et al., 2023), and retrievalaugmented generation (Pan et al., 2024). While other studies focus on educational question generation (Lee et al., 2023;Maity et al., 2025), few target the exploratory nature of virtual simulations (Kurdi et al., 2020), especially third-party labs integrated into teacher-driven lessons, limiting alignment with both interactive context and instructional goals.",
