      "snippet": {
        "text": "The results from this study revealed that PSTs engaged in reflective practices when interpreting and using output from ChatGPT for both pedagogy and mathematics content, but were more accurate in their assessments of output related to pedagogy than they were of mathematical output. These results also revealed important limitations in ChatGPT's usefulness in correctly solving mathematics problems, and for aspects of lesson planning. The emerging literature related to chatbots in educational contexts emphasizes the importance of critique and evaluation of ChatGPT output (Knoth et al., 2024;Trust et al., 2023, van den Berg & du Plessis, 2023;Yilmaz & Yilmaz, 2023), which can be related to the reflective process. In this section, we will first discuss how PSTs reacted to and reflected about ChatGPT's lesson planning suggestions, then the PSTs' reflections and reactions related to ChatGPT's mathematical output, followed by recommendations for the field and limitations. \n\nThe Artzt framework, which was familiar to the PSTs in the study, emphasizes the need for critical reflection across all phases of teaching, including planning, monitoring, and evaluating, and Celik et al. (2022) found that AI can be useful for supporting teachers in each of these phases. Knoth et al. (2024) state the importance of users of AI to have \"the ability, knowledge, and skills to understand, monitor, effectively interact with, and critically reflect on AI-based technologies\" (p. 3), while also noting that it is \"essential for teachers to acquire AI-related skills as part of their digital teaching skills\" (p. 8). The results of this study support these findings. \n\nThe PSTs in this study were able to be reflective and critical about ChatGPT's output as related to pedagogy. In fact, there was evidence that the PSTs analyzed the pedagogical output through the lens of teaching the overarching mathematical tasks of the lesson, most frequently discussing the sequencing and difficulty levels in the lesson, and the motivational strategies (Artzt, 1999).",
