      "snippet": {
        "text": "We evaluate the quality of 123 dialogues from 18 experts by comparing them to real coaching interactions in teacher development programs and find that SimInstruct achieves comparable quality. \n\nOur contributions are as follows: (1) We propose SimInstruct, a novel expert-in-the-loop and responsible data collection tool that leverages LLM-simulated novices to elicit rich, multi-turn instructional dialogues grounded in scaffolding theory. (2) We evaluate the collected dialogues through both qualitative feedback and quantitative analysis. By comparing our data with real expert-novice recordings and fine-tuned LLMs on the augmented dataset, we demonstrate improved instructional quality and pedagogical relevance. (3) We distill practical lessons for involving human experts in high-quality dataset creation and offer actionable insights for building domain-specific AI. \n\nAmong the many domains and subjects where scaffolding is critical, we focus on teacher development as our case study. In both preservice training and ongoing professional learning, expert-novice coaching helps educators interpret classroom events, make instructional decisions, and develop their pedagogical identity [10,28]. Recent research shows that existing tools often fall short in addressing the real-world complexities of teaching, with educators expressing a preference for AI systems that offer more contextualized and reflective support [3]. We answer the research question-How effectively does the use of LLMsimulated novices support the generation of high-quality scaffolding dialogues? \n\n2 Background and Related Work",
