        "text": "The advent of large language models (LLMs) like ChatGPT has generated both excitement and concern in education. On one hand, AI offers unprecedented access to knowledge and rapid content generation, which teachers can leverage for lesson planning, creating learning materials, or receiving tutoring suggestions1 . Studies have found that when used judiciously, generative AI can \"revolutionize classroom dynamics\" by alleviating administrative burdens and providing personalized resources. For example, an autoethnographic study by Kuka and Sabitzer (2024) highlighted AI's pivotal role in reducing a teacher's workload (e.g., drafting curriculum plans), allowing more time for direct student engagement. On the other hand, educators and researchers stress the need for critical oversight: AI-generated content may contain errors or biases, and ethical issues arise regarding transparency (should teachers disclose AI involvement?) and fairness. There is also a psychological dimension -over-reliance on AI or anthropomorphizing it could lead to misplaced trust. Thus, scholarly consensus suggests AI be used as an assistive tool, with educators maintaining \"human-in-the-loop\" control to ensure pedagogical soundness. This perspective aligns with human-computer interaction frameworks like Human-Centered AI, which advocate designing AI systems that augment rather than replace human users.",
