      "snippet": {
        "text": "The growing integration of Generative Artificial Intelligence (GenAI) in educational settings has opened new avenues for innovative assessment methods (Chan and Hu 2023;Tam 2025). Among the most promising applications of GenAI in educational assessment is the use of AI chatbotsautomated conversational agents powered by large language models (LLMs) (Okonkwo and Ade-Ibijola 2021;Chan and Hu 2023). Their ability to process natural language and engage in complex dialogues makes AI chatbots a promising tool for automating various assessment-related tasks, including grading and feedback provision (Okonkwo and Ade-Ibijola 2021;Labadze, Grigolia, and Machaidze 2023). \n\nAI chatbots are often described as virtual teaching assistants, assisting students with coursework, answering questions, and clarifying complex concepts (Fryer, nakao, and Thompson 2019;ding et al. 2023;Labadze, Grigolia, and Machaidze 2023). A particularly valuable feature of AI chatbots is their ability to provide immediate, detailed, and consistent feedback, offering students actionable suggestions to improve their work in real-time (Walter 2024;Usher and Amzalag 2025). This feedback functionality has the potential to enhance formative assessment by helping students refine their understanding, address weaknesses, and iteratively improve their projects or assignments (Chan and Hu 2023;Usher and Amzalag 2025). despite these benefits, concerns persist about the reliability and relevance of AI-generated feedback. AI chatbots may misinterpret student input, producing feedback that is inaccurate or irrelevant to learning objectives (Chan and Hu 2023;nikolopoulou 2024). Furthermore, effective interaction with AI chatbots requires a certain level of AI literacy; the clarity and precision of student prompts largely determine the quality of the chatbot's feedback (nikolopoulou 2024;Tam 2025). Over-reliance on AI-generated feedback also risks diminishing students' development of critical thinking and self-evaluation skills (ding et al. 2023; nguyen et al. 2024). Another challenge is the alignment between AI-generated assessments and traditional human evaluations.",
