      "snippet": {
        "text": "AI has become a powerful tool in education, offering innovative ways to enhance learning experiences [1,10]. Prior research has highlighted the growing influence of GenAI in education [11][12][13]. For instance, AI-based chatbots can be used for personalized learning support [14][15][16]. Still, AI can help to automate tasks -be it administrative [15] or organizational [17] -personalize learning pathways [18], and enhance instructional design [19]. GenAI applications like ChatGPT have demonstrated their ability to create structured lesson plans, provide immediate learner feedback [20], and generate interactive learning content. Studies on the impact of GenAI on coaching and professional training suggest a shift toward blended intelligence, where GenAI serves as an assistant in content generation while human trainers refine and contextualize the material to align with pedagogical goals [21,22]. The role of coaches shifts from direct knowledge transmission to moderating and guiding digitally supported learning processes, a development that [23] describes as 'learnification'. Furthermore, research on anthropomorphism of GenAI indicates that human-like interactions can increase trust but also introduce risks related to over-reliance on AI-generated content [24]. \n\nHowever, despite these advantages, challenges remain, particularly concerning the accuracy and quality of AI-generated content [25]. GenAI may produce misleading or incorrect information [26], which can compromise the reliability of educational content. Trainers and coaches must therefore critically evaluate and refine AI-generated materials to ensure factual accuracy and pedagogical effectiveness [5]. Moreover, the ethical implications of GenAI in educationsuch as bias in AI-generated content -must be considered [27]. The underlying models learn from vast datasets that may contain social biases, potentially perpetuating stereotypes or inaccurate representations in generated learning materials. As a result, trainers and coaches need to approach AI-generated content critically and ensure that it aligns with pedagogical goals and ethical considerations.",
